\title{\vspace{-3.5em}\bfseries An Agentic Loop for Multi-Model Divergence
Hunting on the BOOM Core}
\author{Divya Kohli\\[2pt]
\normalsize University of California, Santa Cruz\\
\normalsize\texttt{dkohli1@ucsc.edu}}
\date{\vspace{-1.2em}\small A3 CHIA Hackathon 2026 --- MICRO A3 Workshop}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
We present a CHIA loop that hunts for divergences between an out-of-order
RISC-V implementation (BOOM) and a functional ISA oracle (Spike) by reading the
implementation's RTL, targeting test programs at predicted disagreement points,
and classifying each observed divergence. The loop is four composable blocks:
\emph{hypothesize} scans BOOM's source for seams where an out-of-order design
must reconstruct behaviour the oracle gets for free; \emph{generate} turns each
hypothesis into a bare-metal test program and refuses any that does not do real
work; \emph{compare} performs a committed-state diff against the oracle; and
\emph{triage} classifies each divergence into one of five categories, deciding
mechanically wherever possible and reserving model judgement for what genuinely
needs it. We report the infrastructure, the design decisions that keep the loop
from reporting noise as bugs, and two concrete artifacts the loop surfaced on
its first real runs: a commit-log convention that manifests as a false
control-flow divergence, and the reproducibility and evidence discipline needed
before any disagreement is called an implementation defect. We are explicit
about what is Berkeley infrastructure we build upon versus what the loop
contributes, and about which results are mechanical versus model-driven.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Comparing a cycle-accurate RTL implementation against a functional ISA
simulator is a standard way to find processor bugs, and the Chipyard ecosystem
already ships the plumbing to do it: the \code{WithCospike} harness attaches
Spike to a BOOM simulation, and \code{WithTraceIO} exposes a commit trace. The
open problem is not \emph{running} the comparison but \emph{driving} it:
deciding what to test, generating stimulus that reaches the interesting corners,
and---above all---deciding what an observed difference \emph{means}. A raw
mismatch between two models is not a bug report. It may be an implementation
defect, an oracle defect, a difference the architecture explicitly permits, or
an artifact of the comparison itself.

This work builds a CHIA loop around exactly that judgement problem. We are
deliberate about attribution: the Spike/BOOM co-simulation capability is
existing Berkeley infrastructure, and we credit it as such. Our contribution is
the agentic layer on top---seam-directed hypothesis generation, a self-checking
stimulus generator, and a triage stage that separates the five outcomes above
with a strong bias against false implementation-defect reports.

\section{Background}
\textbf{CHIA} is an open framework for AI-driven hardware/software co-design
flows, with built-in support for architecture tools including Chipyard, Spike,
Verilator and gem5. \textbf{BOOM} is an out-of-order RISC-V core generator;
\textbf{Spike} is the RISC-V reference ISA simulator. A functional oracle
retires instructions in program order and computes architectural state
directly. An out-of-order implementation reconstructs that same state through
renaming, speculation, queues and bypass networks---the machinery where the two
can disagree. Because the oracle has no notion of cycles, only \emph{committed
architectural state}---the retired PC stream, instruction bits, and
architectural register writes---is a legitimate basis for comparison. Cycle
counts and issue order are not.

\section{Loop Design}
The loop is four blocks, each a self-contained CHIA component.

\subsection{Hypothesize}
The hypothesize block is split into a deterministic stage and a model stage, so
that every hypothesis is traceable to the RTL that motivated it. The
deterministic stage, \code{scan\_seams}, sweeps BOOM's source for markers in
five categories---exception priority, queue-pointer management, bypass/forward
networks, memory ordering, and code the authors themselves flag as unfinished
(\code{TODO}/\code{FIXME} and disabled features). Each hit is recorded with a
\code{file:line} citation. On BOOM v3 (56 Scala files, ${\sim}18.8$k lines) the
scan finds \textbf{423 seams}; Table~\ref{tab:seams} gives the distribution.

The model stage turns ranked seam evidence into structured hypotheses, each with
preconditions, a stimulus sketch, and a predicted observable divergence. A
relevance ranking ensures a memory-ordering hypothesis cites the load/store unit
rather than, say, an instruction-cache fence that merely matched the same
keyword---citations that do not support their claim are worse than no citation.

