\subsection{Generate}
The generate block renders each hypothesis into bare-metal RV64GC assembly,
wrapped in a harness that installs a trap handler (recording \code{mcause} into
committed state and stepping over the faulting instruction) and terminates via
the HTIF \code{tohost} convention. The block's core is a \emph{validation gate}.
Generated assembly fails in ways easily mistaken for results: it may not
assemble, may never terminate, or may terminate having executed nothing. A
program that retires no instructions produces a \emph{clean} comparison, so a
loop that counts clean comparisons as evidence of correctness would happily
report that a broken generator found no bugs. Every program is therefore
assembled, executed on the oracle under an instruction budget, and required to
retire a minimum number of post-reset instructions before entering the suite.
Rejections are counted and reported: they are the gate working, not the loop
failing.

\subsection{Compare}
The compare block normalises both models' output into a single committed-event
stream and diffs them in lockstep from a synchronisation PC, stopping at the
first divergence. Comparison begins at the program's entry rather than at reset,
because the two models execute different boot sequences: on our smoke test BOOM
retires 34 bootrom instructions before entry where Spike retires 5, and
comparing from index zero would report a divergence on every run. A key
practical finding is that BOOM's commit-log fragment emits Spike's exact format
minus a hart prefix, so a single parser serves both; the hart prefix is treated
as optional.

\subsection{Triage}
Triage assigns each divergence to one of five classes: \cls{rtl\_defect},
\cls{model\_defect}, \cls{legal\_nondeterminism}, \cls{harness\_artifact}, or
\cls{inconclusive}. Cases decidable by construction are decided first, with no
model call: a divergence that does not reproduce across repeated runs of two
deterministic models is \cls{inconclusive}; a difference on a free-running
counter CSR (\code{cycle}, \code{time}, \code{instret}) is
\cls{legal\_nondeterminism} by definition; an empty or truncated trace is a
\cls{harness\_artifact}, and we distinguish the two because advising a larger
instruction budget is useless when the simulator never started. Only what
survives this filter is passed to the model, and then with real evidence:
disassembly at both program counters, surrounding trace context, and the RTL
seam the hypothesis cited. The bar for \cls{rtl\_defect} is deliberately high---a
false defect report to BOOM's authors costs more than a missed one---so anything
the evidence does not settle returns \cls{inconclusive} rather than being
rounded up. Only \cls{rtl\_defect} and \cls{model\_defect} findings feed back
into the next hypothesis round; artifacts and legal differences are excluded, as
feeding them back would steer the loop toward more of the same noise.

\section{Implementation}
The loop is ${\sim}3.2$k lines of Python with no third-party runtime
dependencies, driving a toolchain that is fully scripted for reproducible
deployment: Spike (pinned commit \code{0e18353}, built from source),
\code{riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc}~14.2, Verilator~5.022, and a Chipyard BOOM~v3
Verilator simulator built with \code{WithBoomCommitLogPrintf}. The entire
toolchain installs without root privileges via \code{micromamba} plus a
source-built frontend server, so it replays unchanged on a fresh cloud account.
The model interface targets Gemini and degrades to a deterministic offline
baseline when no key is present, so the loop is runnable and testable at zero
API cost; every result carries a \code{provenance} field distinguishing
mechanical, offline-heuristic, and model-driven outcomes. The codebase ships
with 50 unit tests, weighted toward negative controls that confirm the
validation gate and the triage rules reject what they should.

\section{Results}
We report infrastructure results and two qualitative findings; the quantitative
triage-precision and seeded-defect-recall study requires model-driven runs (see
\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\textbf{The loop runs end to end.} From 423 seams, the offline configuration
produces one hypothesis per category, generates five test programs, and all five
clear the validation gate (retiring 20--176 instructions each). Each is executed
on both models and diffed. This exercises every block and the full data path
from RTL scan to classified finding.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering\scriptsize
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Spike (oracle)} & \textbf{BOOM (implementation)} \\
\midrule
\code{0x10 mv a0,s2} & \code{0x10 mv a0,s2} \\
\textit{(handler entry)} & \code{0x12 ecall} $\leftarrow$ \textit{retired} \\
\code{0x50 csrr mcause} & \code{0x50 csrr mcause} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Finding 1. BOOM's commit log retires the trapping \code{ecall}; Spike's
does not. Both reach the handler with identical \code{mcause}. The apparent
control-flow divergence is a logging convention, not a defect.}
\label{fig:finding1}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Finding 1: a commit-log convention masquerading as a control-flow bug.}
Our exception-priority test deliberately traps several times. The comparison
initially reported a \cls{control\_flow} divergence. Investigation---not
assumption---showed that BOOM's commit log retires the trapping instruction
(e.g.\ \code{ecall}) with no architectural writes, whereas Spike's log jumps
directly to the handler entry. Both models then enter the same handler with the
same \code{mcause}; architectural state is identical. This is a commit-log
convention, not a defect. Left unhandled it would fire a false positive on every
exception test. We normalise it---but \emph{count and report} each elision
rather than silently resynchronising, since a differ that quietly realigns can
hide a real bug. Handling it immediately surfaced a second case, the
\emph{compressed} \code{c.ebreak} encoding, which our initial instruction set
missed. This is precisely the implementation-vs-model-vs-artifact distinction the
loop exists to make, produced on a real run.

\textbf{Finding 2: reproducibility and evidence discipline are load-bearing.}
Two bugs in our own harness---a relative ELF path resolved against the wrong
working directory, yielding an empty BOOM trace---were caught by the triage
block, which correctly declined to call the empty trace a defect. This motivated
the mechanical rules that run before any model call: reproduce first, and
distinguish ``the tool did not run'' from ``the models disagree.''

\section{Limitations and Future Work}
\label{sec:limitations}
The central limitation is honest and specific: the judgement calls are stubbed.
Without an API key, every divergence needing real analysis is returned
\cls{inconclusive} rather than guessed, and the offline hypotheses and test
programs are coarse category templates. The headline metrics our design is built
to produce---triage precision against manual review, and recall against a set of
defects deliberately injected into BOOM's RTL as a control---require
model-driven runs on the funded compute. The mutation machinery for the seeded
control set is reused from our formal-verification prototype and applies to
BOOM's Verilog without change. A second axis, adding a second independent oracle
(e.g.\ Dromajo, already supported by Chipyard) would let triage distinguish
implementation from oracle defects mechanically: disagreement with both oracles
implicates the implementation, disagreement with one implicates that oracle.

\section{Conclusion}
We have built a complete, reproducible agentic loop for multi-model divergence
hunting on BOOM, with the co-simulation credited as Berkeley infrastructure and
the agentic hypothesize/generate/triage layer as the contribution. The design's
distinguishing feature is its refusal to manufacture findings: a validation gate
that rejects work-free programs, mechanical triage rules that resolve the cheap
cases for free, and a high, evidence-gated bar for reporting an implementation
defect. On its first runs the loop already produced a genuine
implementation-versus-artifact distinction. With model-driven triage on the
funded compute, the same scaffolding yields the precision and recall numbers the
approach is designed to measure.

